@*
    What the reader is looking for, in a card above the lists.

    Above rather than inside the table, and deliberately: a filter row wedged into a header is a row
    that fights the columns for width, disappears when its column is hidden, and has nowhere to put
    a control that narrows something the table is not currently showing. These narrow the empires,
    not the columns, so they sit with the question rather than with the answer.

    One set for both lists. "Which of these are Fanatic Purifiers" is not a question about the
    player's empires or about the game's, and answering it twice with two sets of controls would be
    two chances to have them disagree.

    Written from the table of headings rather than one block per heading. There are thirteen, every
    one is the same control asking the same question of a different field, and thirteen copies is
    thirteen chances for one of them to be wired to the wrong set.
*@

<div class="sem-filters @(_open ? "open" : null)">
    <div class="head">
        @*
            The heading is the way in, and the way back out. The card starts shut: twenty-eight
            controls in four tabs is a wall between the reader and the empires they came for, and
            every one of them is a question they have not asked yet.
        *@
        <button type="button" class="fold" aria-expanded="@(_open ? "true" : "false")"
                @onclick="() => _open = !_open">
            <span class="chevron" aria-hidden="true">@(_open ? "▾" : "▸")</span>
            <h2>Empire Filter</h2>
        </button>

        @if (Filter.Any)
        {
            @*
                Outside the fold, so a filter somebody folded away can still be cleared - and so a
                card that is narrowing the lists says so while it is shut.
            *@
            <button type="button" class="sem-clear" @onclick="Clear">Clear</button>
        }
    </div>

    <div class="body">
        @*
            Twenty-eight headings in four tabs. All of them at once was a wall to read rather than a
            card to use.

            The search box used to sit above them, and folding the card away - which is what a
            reader did about the wall, and is now what the card does by itself - took the broadest
            control in it out of reach. It lives on the table's heading row instead, where nothing
            can fold it away.

            The funnel says a tab has something set under it. Without it, folding twenty-one
            headings out of sight is a good way to leave a filter on and spend a while wondering why
            the list is short.

            aria-pressed rather than role="tab", as the view toggles and the picker tabs here
            already do: a real tablist owes the reader arrow-key movement between its tabs, and a
            group of buttons that each say whether they are pressed owes nothing it does not give.
        *@
        <div class="sem-filter-tabs" role="group" aria-label="Which filters to show">
            @foreach (var group in EmpireFacet.Groups)
            {
                var set = Narrowing(group);

                <button type="button" aria-pressed="@(group == _group ? "true" : "false")"
                        title="@(set ? $"{group} - something is set here" : group)"
                        @onclick="() => _group = group">
                    <span>@group</span>

                    @if (set)
                    {
                        <ActionIcon Name="filter" />
                    }
                </button>
            }
        </div>

    <div class="controls">
        @foreach (var facet in EmpireFacet.All.Where(f => f.Group == _group))
        {
            var options = Offered(facet);

            <div class="sem-field flush">
                <label>@facet.Label</label>

                @*
                    Two answers and a way of asking neither, which is a dropdown rather than a list
                    of ticks: ticking both boxes and ticking neither meant the same thing, and a
                    control with two ways to say All is a control that has to be worked out.
                *@
                @if (facet.YesNo)
                {
                    <select value="@Answer(facet)"
                            @onchange="e => Answering(facet, e.Value?.ToString())">
                        <option value="">All</option>
                        <option value="yes">Yes</option>
                        <option value="no">No</option>
                    </select>
                }
                else
                {
                <MultiSelect Label="Any" Options="@options" Selected="@Filter.Keys(facet.Key)"
                             OnChanged="OnChanged">
                    <Leading>
                        @*
                            Any or all, for a heading an empire can hold several of, and inside the
                            control it acts on rather than floating above it: it is a question about
                            this list, and a row of its own said it was about something else.

                            Two overlapping rings, shaded to say which part is being asked for. Not
                            offered where an empire holds one - asking for all of two authorities is
                            a question with no answer, and a control that can only ever empty the
                            list is a control that looks broken.
                        *@
                        @if (facet.Several)
                        {
                            <button type="button" class="mode" aria-label="@Switching(facet)"
                                    aria-pressed="@(Filter.RequiresAll(facet.Key) ? "true" : "false")"
                                    title="@Switching(facet)"
                                    @onclick="() => Switch(facet.Key)" @onclick:stopPropagation="true">
                                <ActionIcon Name="@(Filter.RequiresAll(facet.Key) ? "all" : "any")" />
                            </button>
                        }
                    </Leading>
                </MultiSelect>
                }
            </div>
        }
    </div>
    </div>
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// Every empire there is, both lists together, which is what the choices are drawn from.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Offered rather than exhaustive: the game has eighty-one civics and a file has a handful, and
    /// a list of eighty-one where seventy-four find nothing is a list to scroll past rather than to
    /// read. What is here is what something in one of the two lists actually holds.
    /// </remarks>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public required IReadOnlyList<EmpireRow> Rows { get; set; }

    [CascadingParameter]
    public required DesignSession Session { get; set; }

    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public required EmpireFilter Filter { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Raised whenever the filter changed, so the page can draw the lists again.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback OnChanged { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The choices under each heading, worked out once for the rows in hand.</summary>
    private readonly Dictionary<string, IReadOnlyList<EmpireChoice>> _offered =
        new(StringComparer.Ordinal);

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether the card is showing its controls, which to begin with it is not.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Shut on every window. It used to open wherever there was room, which meant asking the
    /// browser how wide it was before the card could be drawn - and that answer only ever shut the
    /// card, never opened one that was already open, so starting shut says the same thing on a
    /// phone and says it without the round trip.
    ///
    /// Shut is also the right state on a desktop, which is the part the width was answering
    /// wrongly. The reader arriving at the list has not asked any of these questions yet, and a
    /// card of them standing between the page and the empires is answered by folding it away -
    /// which is what the fold was for, and what a reader had to do first every time.
    /// </remarks>
    private bool _open;

    /// <summary>Which tab of headings is showing.</summary>
    private string _group = EmpireFacet.Groups[0];

    /// <summary>Whether anything under one tab is narrowing the lists, so the tab can say so.</summary>
    private bool Narrowing(string group) =>
        EmpireFacet.All.Any(f => f.Group == group && Filter.Keys(f.Key).Count > 0);

    /// <summary>What the editor would offer under each heading, worked out once and held.</summary>
    private EmpireOptions? _options;

    /// <summary>The lists are worked out from the rows, so a change of rows forgets them.</summary>
    protected override void OnParametersSet()
    {
        _offered.Clear();
        _options = new EmpireOptions(Session);
    }

    /// <summary>What the any-or-all switch is doing, said the same way in the label and the tip.</summary>
    private string Switching(EmpireFacet facet) =>
        Filter.RequiresAll(facet.Key)
            ? $"Asking for empires with every one of the {facet.Label.ToLowerInvariant()} ticked"
            : $"Asking for empires with any of the {facet.Label.ToLowerInvariant()} ticked";

    /// <summary>Everything the rows hold under one heading, once each, in the order it reads in.</summary>
    private IReadOnlyList<EmpireChoice> Offered(EmpireFacet facet)
    {
        if (_offered.TryGetValue(facet.Key, out var known))
        {
            return known;
        }

        // What the editor would offer, and then whatever these empires hold on top of it. The
        // second half is not redundant: the offer is made for an empire with nothing chosen yet,
        // and a few shelves narrow as choices are made - the traits a machine may take are not the
        // traits a mammal may. Anything a listed empire actually holds is by definition something
        // an empire may hold, so it belongs in the list whether or not the blank one was offered it.
        IReadOnlyList<EmpireChoice> options =
        [
            .. (facet.Fixed is { } offered ? offered(_options!) : [])
                .Concat(Rows.SelectMany(facet.Values))
                .DistinctBy(c => c.Key, StringComparer.Ordinal)
                .OrderBy(c => c.Name, StringComparer.CurrentCulture)
        ];

        _offered[facet.Key] = options;
        return options;
    }

    /// <summary>Which of the three a yes-or-no heading is set to, as the dropdown spells it.</summary>
    private string Answer(EmpireFacet facet)
    {
        var keys = Filter.Keys(facet.Key);

        return keys.Contains("yes") ? "yes" : keys.Contains("no") ? "no" : string.Empty;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Sets a yes-or-no heading, All being the absence of an answer rather than a third one.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Written into the same set every other heading uses, so the matching does not have to know
    /// that these two are drawn differently from the rest.
    /// </remarks>
    private async Task Answering(EmpireFacet facet, string? answer)
    {
        var keys = Filter.Keys(facet.Key);
        keys.Clear();

        if (answer is { Length: > 0 })
        {
            keys.Add(answer);
        }

        await OnChanged.InvokeAsync();
    }

    private async Task Switch(string facet)
    {
        Filter.SetRequiresAll(facet, !Filter.RequiresAll(facet));
        await OnChanged.InvokeAsync();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Puts every heading back to asking for nothing, and empties the search box with them.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// The box is not in this card any more, and clearing it from here is still right: it narrows
    /// the same list, it is the reason this button is showing whenever nothing else is set, and a
    /// Clear that left a word behind would leave a reader looking at a short list with nothing on
    /// screen claiming to have shortened it.
    /// </remarks>
    private async Task Clear()
    {
        Filter.Clear();
        await OnChanged.InvokeAsync();
    }
}
